### When to Break from Tables

NoSQL databases trade relational guarantees for flexibility and horizontal scale. MongoDB is the most popular document database.

#### Documents vs Rows

```js
// SQL: normalized, split across tables
users:   { id: 1, name: 'Alice' }
addresses: { user_id: 1, street: '123 Main St', city: 'NYC' }

// MongoDB: embedded document — no join needed
{
  _id: ObjectId("..."),
  name: "Alice",
  address: {
    street: "123 Main St",
    city: "NYC"
  },
  tags: ["premium", "verified"]
}
```

#### MongoDB CRUD

```js
// Insert
await db.users.insertOne({ name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' });

// Find
await db.users.find({ city: 'NYC' }).sort({ name: 1 }).limit(10).toArray();

// Update
await db.users.updateOne(
  { _id: userId },
  { $set: { name: 'Alice Smith' }, $push: { tags: 'admin' } }
);

// Delete
await db.users.deleteOne({ _id: userId });

// Aggregation pipeline
await db.orders.aggregate([
  { $match: { status: 'completed' } },
  { $group: { _id: '$userId', total: { $sum: '$amount' } } },
  { $sort: { total: -1 } },
  { $limit: 10 }
]).toArray();
```

#### SQL vs NoSQL: When to Choose What

| Scenario | Choose |
| :--- | :--- |
| Complex relationships, financial data | SQL (PostgreSQL) |
| Flexible schema, hierarchical data | MongoDB |
| Simple key lookups, caching | Redis |
| Full-text search | Elasticsearch |

